In news:<42ba0b7b$0$14321$c3e8da3@news.astraweb.com>, anon 
<anon@anon.com> says...
> After losing two more wizards this week to a giant mimic and a rope
> golem, I'm wondering what is the best way to survive a grabbing attack.
> I always start my wizards out with sleep spell, but rope golems are
> sleep resistant, and sleep spell is often useless in close spaces where
> giant mimics are usually found.
> So what is my best option (besides not being grabbed in the first
> place)? Writing Elbereth? (which *never* seems to work when your
> miserable life actually depends on it) Reading a scroll of teleport?
> Prayer? Or are wizards basically screwed once they get grabbed?

Magic cancellation protects against mimics' "sticky" attack. This is not 
a complete protection, but it greatly reduces the chance of the attacks 
succeeding. Get magic cancellation level 3.

Not that I think there is ever a reason for a careful player to have a 
problem with mimics, like many others have said in this thread.

Magic cancellation does not help against the grabbing attacks of rope 
golems, owlbears, etc - AFAIK.

